Reasoning for AsynIO:
1.I/O Bound Nature: API calls are I/O bound. The CPU spends 99% of its time waiting for the network.
2.Efficiency: asyncio uses a single-threaded event loop. It "pauses" a task when it hits an await (like a request) and moves to the next one.
3.Overhead: threading requires the OS to manage context-switching between thousands of threads, each with its own memory stack. asyncio is much lighter, allowing you to handle thousands of connections concurrently on a single thread.
4.The GIL: While threading does bypass the Global Interpreter Lock during I/O, asyncio is generally the modern standard for high-concurrency network tasks in Python.

threading vs  asyncio
Pre-emptive multitasking (OS switches) vs Cooperative multitasking (Task yields)
Complexity -> High (Race conditions, Locks) vs Medium (Need to understand await/async)
Scalability -> Limited by OS thread limits vs Very high (10k+ connections possible)
Library Support -> Works with standard requests vs Requires async-compatible libs (httpx)

"If you use asyncio, but the library you are forced to use is synchronous (like a legacy database driver), what happens to your performance?"
The "Senior" Answer: "The event loop will block. One slow synchronous call will stop the entire loop, effectively making the program run one-at-a-time unless I wrap that specific call in a thread pool using loop.run_in_executor()."


Why the sequential version is slow
- Each API call blocks until the previous one finishes.
- Network I/O is mostly waiting, not computing.
- Total time ≈ sum of all request latencies.

Multithreading
- Good for moderate concurrency (10–100 threads).
- Works with standard requests (library support inbuilt)

Async Event Loop
- No threads → no OS context switching.  Better scaling. upto 10k
- Little bit complex
- Requires AIO compatible libs (httpx or aiohttp)

